// @korulang/unikraft-alloc — Unikraft's object allocator (`ukalloc`), lifted.
//
//   import unikraft/alloc
//
// WHAT THIS IS. `lib/ukalloc` is the layer every other Unikraft library gets its
// memory from. The brief that commissioned this lift names it "a genuinely
// symmetric alloc/free pair; a good test of *not* over-modelling", and the grep
// agrees: **38 `UK_ASSERT`s across `alloc.c`, `stats.c` and `alloc.h`, of which
// 29 are the single expression `UK_ASSERT(a)`**. That is an existence rule, not
// an ordering rule. There is no INVALID -> UNCONFIGURED -> RUNNING here and
// there is nothing to transcribe. `unikraft/blk` needed an eight-state ratchet
// because ukblkdev has a state machine; ukalloc does not.
//
// So this module carries exactly TWO states, and the larger part of the work was
// deciding what NOT to model. Every refusal is argued in README.md rather than
// left as an absence.
//
//     take          mints  <raw!>                       uk_malloc_ifpages
//     take.zeroed   mints  <live!>                      uk_malloc_ifpages + memset
//     take.aligned  mints  <raw!>                       uk_posix_memalign_ifpages
//     write         needs  <!raw|!live>  mints <live!>  (no C call)
//     read          needs  <!live>       mints <live!>  (no C call)
//     resize        needs  <!live>       mints <live!>  uk_realloc_ifpages
//     untouched     needs  <!raw>        mints <live!>  (NO C CALL — the escape)
//     free          needs  <!live>                      uk_free_ifpages
//
// `raw` is memory you own whose contents are garbage. `live` is memory you own
// whose contents you put there. The distinction is not invented: it is the
// difference between the doc comment on `uk_malloc` (alloc.h:150 — "Allocate an
// object of `size` bytes", nothing about contents) and the one on `uk_calloc`
// (alloc.h:176 — "and zero out its memory").
//
// THE ASYMMETRY (pillar 4), and it does two jobs for the price of one state.
//
//   1. `free` accepts ONLY <!live>. So
//
//          take(heap, bytes: 64) | block b |> free(b)
//
//      does not compile. Allocating memory and handing it straight back is a
//      no-op you cannot write. The C has no opinion about this at all —
//      `malloc(); free();` is warning-free, sanitizer-clean and does no work.
//      This is the one place the lift keeps a guarantee Unikraft never had
//      rather than transcribing one it wrote.
//
//   2. `read` accepts ONLY <!live>. So
//
//          take(heap, bytes: 64) | block b |> read(b, at: 0, len: 64)
//
//      does not compile either. `uk_malloc_ifpages` hands back a slice of a
//      buddy-allocated page whose previous tenant's bytes are still in it
//      (alloc.c:154-176 writes nothing but the 32-byte metadata header BEFORE
//      the returned pointer). In C that read is legal, defined nowhere, and the
//      bug class MSan exists for. Here it costs zero extra states.
//
// Both are the memory twin of gzip's `fed` gate (koru-libs/gzip/index.kz:258,
// where `finish` accepts only <!fed> and only `push` mints it) and of the
// transaction showcase's `exec`-gates-`commit` rule (koru
// tests/.../2104_14_open_tx_commit_close/db.kz, where `close` takes <!active>
// and not <!connected>).
//
// THE ESCAPE IS NAMED, NOT DEFAULT: `unikraft/alloc:untouched`. It runs no C
// call, mints <live!> from <raw>, and exists because reserving memory you never
// write to is legitimate — a heap probe, a benchmark warm-up, a reservation held
// so a later allocation cannot take the run. The point of naming it is the grep:
//
//     grep -r "alloc:untouched"   ->   "where do we reserve memory we never write to"
//
// A permissive `free` that also took <!raw> cannot answer that question, and a
// strict `free` plus a named escape CONTAINS the permissive design — the
// programs a permissive `free` accepts are exactly the ones this module accepts
// with one extra word in them. That asymmetry is why the strict reading wins.
//
// WHY `untouched` AND NOT `unused`. "Unused" reads as *finished with*, which is
// the state a block is in immediately BEFORE `free` and is precisely the wrong
// suggestion at a call site whose whole meaning is "I never wrote here".
// `untouched` names the fact being asserted: no byte of this region was written
// by this program. The grep is the interface; it should read as the claim.
//
// WHERE THIS LIFT DELIBERATELY MODELS NOTHING.
//
//   * THE ALLOCATOR HANDLE carries no phantom state. `uk_alloc_register`
//     (alloc.c:56) has no inverse — there is no unregister, an allocator is
//     never given back — so an obligation on it could never be discharged, and
//     the tor discharging it would emit nothing. The 29 `UK_ASSERT(a)`s are
//     still dissolved, because the only way to NAME a `*Heap` is to be inside
//     the `| ready` arm of `default`. The proof rides on the BRANCH, not on a
//     state. State is not the only thing that can carry a proof, and reaching
//     for it when a branch already does is how state counts get to fourteen.
//
//   * `available` takes and mints nothing. Knowing how much memory is free
//     constrains nothing about what you may do next.
//
//   * ALIGNMENT IS NOT A STATE. `take.aligned` mints the same <raw!> as `take`.
//     Alignment is a property of the address, not of what you may legally do
//     next, and the brief's rule is that state answers "what may I do now".
//
//   * `uk_palloc` / `uk_pfree` IS NOT HERE. It is a different C entry point with
//     a different unit and a splittable release rule (alloc.h:344-347: "pages
//     obtained in a single palloc may be freed through any combination of pfree
//     calls, as long as each page gets freed exactly once"). It lives in
//     `unikraft/pages`, which is the catalog's stated exception to one-module-
//     per-sublibrary.
//
// WHY `default` HAS TWO FAILURE ARMS AND NOT ONE. `uk_malloc` with a NULL
// allocator does not assert — it does `errno = ENOMEM; return __NULL;`
// (alloc.h:161-164), which at the call site is indistinguishable from a genuine
// out-of-memory. "Boot never registered an allocator" and "the heap is full" are
// different problems with different fixes, and the C reports them as the same
// value. That is a silent fallback. Here they are `| unavailable` (on `default`)
// and `| exhausted` (on `take`), and no program can conflate them.
//
// FAILURE ARMS hand the block back so the obligation cannot be dropped:
//
//     take          exhausted -> no block at all; there is nothing to own
//     take.aligned  refused   -> no block at all, plus the POSITIVE errno
//     write         rejected  -> <raw!>   nothing was copied
//     read          rejected  -> <live!>  nothing was read; the block is untouched
//     resize        refused   -> <live!>  the ORIGINAL block, still yours, still owed
//
// `write`'s refusal is the one arm that loses a proof: nothing was copied, and
// the lift deliberately does not remember whether the block was `live` on the
// way in, so it comes back `raw`. That is a loss of proof, not a lie — `raw`
// means "not provably initialized", which is true of a block this call did not
// touch. If a refused write minted <live!>, then take + an out-of-range write +
// free would be a hole straight through the asymmetry gate.
//
// WHY `resize`'s FAILURE ARM IS THE HEADLINE. The C signature makes the
// canonical leak the path of least resistance: `p = uk_realloc(a, p, n)` on
// failure overwrites the only pointer to the still-live old block with NULL.
// ukalloc has no assertion for this and cannot have one. Here `resize` consumes
// the block, so the pre-resize binding is dead by construction, and the
// `| refused` arm is the only place the original can come back — named, owned,
// and impossible to drop silently. See tests/negative_use_after_resize.kz.
//
// ONE HONEST LIMIT, stated up front: `<live>` is per-BLOCK, not per-byte. One
// `write` makes the written prefix readable and no more — `read` is bounded by
// `used`, not by `cap` — but no phantom state can be per-byte. What the gate
// catches is the block that was never written at all, and empirically that is
// the shape the bug takes.

// ============================================================================
// UNIKRAFT C ABI
// ============================================================================
//
// Linkability, checked the way the linker checks it. `lib/ukalloc/exportsyms.uk`
// EXISTS, so this is case 1 of the brief's three-case rule: that file is the
// allowlist, 25 lines long, and everything else is localized by
// `objcopy --keep-global-symbols`. Every symbol below is on it.
//
// The trap in this target, and the whole reason the shelf's row reads
// "linkable 25 / static inline 25": every user-facing entry point of ukalloc —
// `uk_malloc`, `uk_calloc`, `uk_zalloc`, `uk_realloc`, `uk_free`, `uk_memalign`,
// `uk_posix_memalign`, `uk_palloc`, `uk_pfree`, `uk_alloc_availmem`,
// `uk_alloc_maxalloc`, `uk_alloc_get_default` — is `static inline` in
// `include/uk/alloc.h`, dispatching through the `struct uk_alloc` vtable. A
// separately-linked Koru archive cannot call one of them. Case 1 and case 3
// apply to the same library at once.
//
// The way through is NOT a shim and NOT an ABI guess, and this module mirrors
// NO STRUCT AT ALL. `uk_alloc_init_palloc` (alloc_impl.h:260-281) installs
// `uk_malloc_ifpages`, `uk_realloc_ifpages`, `uk_posix_memalign_ifpages` and
// `uk_free_ifpages` as `a->malloc`, `a->realloc`, `a->posix_memalign` and
// `a->free` for every page-backed allocator — including `ukallocbbuddy`, the
// default in every standard boot (bbuddy.c:670). All four ARE exported. So
// calling them directly on the default allocator is the same code the vtable
// would have reached, minus one indirect call. `struct uk_alloc` is never
// mirrored and never dereferenced; the handle stays opaque from acquisition to
// free.
//
// (`unikraft/pages` DOES mirror the table, because `palloc`/`pfree` have no
// exported implementation to call. It proves the mirror against six exported
// symbol addresses before it uses it. That cost buys nothing here, so it is not
// paid here.)
//
// If the default allocator does not provide the page interface,
// `uk_malloc_ifpages` returns NULL and `take` reports `| exhausted`. That is a
// loud failure on the first allocation, not a silent fallback, and no `free` is
// reachable because no block was ever minted.

const UkAlloc = opaque {};

// `uk_alloc_get_default()` is `static inline` and returns exactly this exported
// global (alloc.h:136-139, exportsyms.uk:2 and :22). Reading the variable is the
// same load the inline function performs. It is NULL until `ukboot` registers an
// allocator (alloc.c:54, :60).
extern var _uk_alloc_head: ?*UkAlloc;

extern fn uk_malloc_ifpages(a: *UkAlloc, size: usize) ?*anyopaque;
extern fn uk_realloc_ifpages(a: *UkAlloc, ptr: ?*anyopaque, size: usize) ?*anyopaque;
extern fn uk_posix_memalign_ifpages(a: *UkAlloc, memptr: *?*anyopaque, alignment: usize, size: usize) c_int;
extern fn uk_free_ifpages(a: *UkAlloc, ptr: ?*anyopaque) void;
extern fn uk_alloc_availmem_total() usize;

// The errnos ukalloc itself returns from `uk_posix_memalign_ifpages`
// (alloc.c:245-247, :255). POSITIVE, per POSIX — that call is the one entry
// point in the library that does not use the negative-errno convention, and
// getting it wrong by hand turns a failed allocation into a successful-looking
// one. The arm carries the sign the C used.
const EINVAL: i32 = 22;
const ENOMEM: i32 = 12;

// ============================================================================
// THE HANDLE AND THE BLOCK
// ============================================================================

// The default allocator, opaque. No phantom state — see the header note.
//
// Named `Heap` and not `Allocator` because a module-local type named
// `Allocator` is silently rewritten to the compiler's own `__koru_std.mem.
// Allocator` in the emitted Zig. Pinned in README.md as toolchain defect 1.
const Heap = opaque {};

// A `Block` header lives at the FRONT of its own allocation, so one `take` is
// one C call and never two. It carries the allocator it came from, which is what
// makes "free with the wrong allocator" — a rule alloc.h:299 states in prose and
// never checks anywhere — unspellable: `free` has no allocator parameter to get
// wrong.
//
// `used` is how many bytes the caller has actually written. `read` is bounded by
// it rather than by `cap`, so a partial `write` does not silently promise that
// the tail is readable.
//
// `off` is where the caller's region starts relative to the header, and
// `alignment` is 0 for a plain block or the requested alignment for one from
// `take.aligned`. Those two fields are what let ONE header layout serve both,
// and they are `u32` on purpose: the struct then fits exactly in HEADER with no
// growth over an unaligned-only design.
const Block = extern struct {
    heap: *UkAlloc,
    cap: usize,
    used: usize,
    off: u32,
    alignment: u32,
};

// The user region of a plain block starts HEADER bytes in. The constant is a
// power of two at least as large as the header for exactly the reason
// `METADATA_IFPAGES_SIZE_POW2` is (alloc.c:84-97): a few wasted bytes are
// negligible, an alignment break is not. `uk_malloc_ifpages` returns
// `page_start + 32` (alloc.c:176), so `+ 32` again keeps the caller's region
// 32-byte aligned — stricter than `max_align_t` on every supported target.
const HEADER: usize = 32;

// An alignment this lift will accept. Anything larger cannot be stored in the
// `u32` header field, and a refusal is the only honest answer — silently
// clamping an alignment is exactly the class of fallback this shelf forbids.
const MAX_ALIGNMENT: u64 = 1 << 30;

comptime {
    if (@sizeOf(Block) > HEADER) @compileError("unikraft/alloc: Block header outgrew HEADER");
    if (HEADER % 16 != 0) @compileError("unikraft/alloc: HEADER must keep max_align_t alignment");
}

fn alignUp(n: usize, a: usize) usize {
    return (n + a - 1) & ~(a - 1);
}

fn regionOf(b: *Block) [*]u8 {
    const base: [*]u8 = @ptrCast(b);
    return base + b.off;
}

fn publish(a: *UkAlloc, raw: *anyopaque, off: usize, cap: usize, used: usize, alignment: usize) *Block {
    const b: *Block = @ptrCast(@alignCast(raw));
    b.* = Block{
        .heap = a,
        .cap = cap,
        .used = used,
        .off = @intCast(off),
        .alignment = @intCast(alignment),
    };
    return b;
}

// ============================================================================
// ACQUIRE THE ALLOCATOR
// ============================================================================

// The default allocator, or an explicit refusal.
//
// This one tor is what dissolves the `UK_ASSERT(a)` rule: downstream, a `*Heap`
// exists only inside the `| ready` arm, so there is no program in which an
// allocation is attempted against an allocator that was never registered. That
// rule has 29 sites; README.md attributes each one and counts 14 of them as
// retired by this module, because the other 15 sit on entry points this module
// does not bind — and not offering a feature is not retiring its check.
//
// It also removes the silent fallback described in the header note: with a NULL
// allocator, ukalloc reports "no allocator" and "out of memory" as the same
// NULL. Here they are `| unavailable` and `| exhausted`.
~pub tor default { }
| ready *Heap
| unavailable string

~proc default|zig {
    const a = _uk_alloc_head orelse {
        return .{ .unavailable = "no allocator registered: ukboot has not run, or CONFIG_LIBUKALLOC is off" };
    };
    return .{ .ready = @ptrCast(a) };
}

// Total free memory across every registered allocator, in bytes.
//
// `uk_alloc_availmem_total` (alloc.c:594-603) walks `_uk_alloc_head` itself and
// needs no handle, and it is a REAL exported symbol — so this reading costs no
// ABI claim and no vtable read. No obligation: knowing how much is free
// constrains nothing about what you may do next.
//
// This is the reading the boot test brackets the whole program with. Two equal
// numbers on the console are the runtime proof that nothing leaked; the
// compiler's guarantees are about what can be written, and this is about what
// actually ran.
~pub tor available { } -> u64

~proc available|zig {
    return @intCast(uk_alloc_availmem_total());
}

// ============================================================================
// ALLOCATE
// ============================================================================

// `uk_malloc`, and the block comes back <raw!> — yours, contents undefined.
//
// The emitted call is `uk_malloc_ifpages(heap, HEADER + bytes)`: one C call, the
// same one `uk_malloc` would have dispatched to.
//
// A zero-byte request is REFUSED rather than passed through. `uk_malloc_ifpages`
// answers `!size` with NULL (alloc.c:156-157), which the caller would then read
// as out-of-memory. Two different problems must not share one report.
~pub tor take { heap: *Heap, bytes: u64 }
| block *Block<raw!>
| exhausted { bytes: u64, reason: string }

~proc take|zig {
    if (bytes == 0) {
        return .{ .exhausted = .{ .bytes = bytes, .reason = "zero-byte allocation: uk_malloc returns NULL for size 0 and calls it out of memory (alloc.c:156)" } };
    }
    const want: usize = @intCast(bytes);
    const total = HEADER + want;
    if (total < want) {
        return .{ .exhausted = .{ .bytes = bytes, .reason = "allocation size overflows the address space once the block header is added" } };
    }
    const a: *UkAlloc = @ptrCast(@constCast(heap));
    const raw = uk_malloc_ifpages(a, total) orelse {
        return .{ .exhausted = .{ .bytes = bytes, .reason = "uk_malloc_ifpages returned NULL" } };
    };
    return .{ .block = publish(a, raw, HEADER, want, 0, 0) };
}

// `uk_calloc`, and the block comes back <live!> — every byte is a zero this call
// put there, so reading it is honest.
//
// This is the ONE way past the read-before-write gate that is not an escape
// hatch, because it is a different C function that really does write the memory.
// A caller who wants a readable buffer without writing one asks for a zeroed
// one, and pays the memset that makes the claim true.
//
// `uk_calloc_compat` is exported but reaches `a->malloc` through the vtable and
// knows nothing about this lift's header, so the zeroing is done here. What is
// emitted is `uk_malloc_ifpages` plus the same memset `uk_calloc_compat` does
// (alloc.c:562), minus one indirect call.
~pub tor take.zeroed { heap: *Heap, bytes: u64 }
| block *Block<live!>
| exhausted { bytes: u64, reason: string }

~proc take.zeroed|zig {
    if (bytes == 0) {
        return .{ .exhausted = .{ .bytes = bytes, .reason = "zero-byte allocation: uk_calloc returns NULL for size 0 and calls it out of memory (alloc.c:548)" } };
    }
    const want: usize = @intCast(bytes);
    const total = HEADER + want;
    if (total < want) {
        return .{ .exhausted = .{ .bytes = bytes, .reason = "allocation size overflows the address space once the block header is added" } };
    }
    const a: *UkAlloc = @ptrCast(@constCast(heap));
    const raw = uk_malloc_ifpages(a, total) orelse {
        return .{ .exhausted = .{ .bytes = bytes, .reason = "uk_malloc_ifpages returned NULL" } };
    };
    const b = publish(a, raw, HEADER, want, want, 0);
    const region = regionOf(b);
    for (0..want) |i| region[i] = 0;
    return .{ .block = b };
}

// `uk_posix_memalign`. The reason a unikernel application talks to ukalloc
// directly at all: a DMA buffer has an alignment the device dictates.
//
// THE HEADER PROBLEM, AND WHY IT IS SOLVED RATHER THAN AVOIDED. A `Block` header
// at the front of an alignment-constrained region looks impossible — the header
// pushes the payload off the boundary. It is not: ask for
// `alignUp(HEADER, alignment)` bytes of header instead of HEADER. The base comes
// back `alignment`-aligned from `uk_posix_memalign_ifpages`, the header size is
// a multiple of `alignment`, so `base + off` is `alignment`-aligned too. One C
// call, no side table, no second allocation.
//
// THE COST, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN: for `alignment > 32` the header is a full
// `alignment` bytes, so a 4096-aligned request wastes 4096 bytes. That is real
// and it is the price of a stable handle. The alternative designs — a side table
// or a header behind the region — trade it for a second allocation or a second
// pointer chase on every access, and this shelf's pillar 2 is about the emitted
// call sequence.
//
// `refused` carries the POSITIVE errno the C returns. The alignment rules are
// checked here as well as in the C (alloc.c:245-247), not as a duplicated guard
// but because the header arithmetic above needs a proven power of two.
~pub tor take.aligned { heap: *Heap, bytes: u64, alignment: u64 }
| block *Block<raw!>
| refused { errno: i32, bytes: u64, reason: string }

~proc take.aligned|zig {
    if (bytes == 0) {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .errno = EINVAL, .bytes = bytes, .reason = "zero-byte aligned allocation: uk_posix_memalign answers size 0 with EINVAL (alloc.c:255)" } };
    }
    if (alignment == 0 or (alignment & (alignment - 1)) != 0) {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .errno = EINVAL, .bytes = bytes, .reason = "alignment must be a power of two" } };
    }
    if (alignment % @sizeOf(usize) != 0) {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .errno = EINVAL, .bytes = bytes, .reason = "alignment must be a multiple of the pointer size" } };
    }
    if (alignment > MAX_ALIGNMENT) {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .errno = EINVAL, .bytes = bytes, .reason = "alignment larger than 1 GiB does not fit this lift's block header, and clamping it silently would be a lie about the address" } };
    }
    const al: usize = @intCast(alignment);
    const want: usize = @intCast(bytes);
    const off = alignUp(HEADER, al);
    const total = off + want;
    if (total < want) {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .errno = EINVAL, .bytes = bytes, .reason = "allocation size overflows the address space once the aligned block header is added" } };
    }

    const a: *UkAlloc = @ptrCast(@constCast(heap));
    var out: ?*anyopaque = null;
    const rc = uk_posix_memalign_ifpages(a, &out, al, total);
    if (rc != 0) {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .errno = @intCast(rc), .bytes = bytes, .reason = "uk_posix_memalign_ifpages refused the aligned request" } };
    }
    const raw = out orelse {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .errno = ENOMEM, .bytes = bytes, .reason = "uk_posix_memalign_ifpages reported success with a NULL pointer" } };
    };
    return .{ .block = publish(a, raw, off, want, 0, al) };
}

// ============================================================================
// USE
// ============================================================================

// Copy `bytes` into the block from offset 0 and mint <live!>.
//
// No C call: ukalloc hands out memory, it does not touch contents. This tor
// exists because it is what makes <live!> true, and because it is the only thing
// that can.
//
// A refusal hands the block back as <raw!>. Nothing was copied, so the lift
// cannot prove the contents are initialized — and it deliberately does not
// remember whether they were before the call. See the header note on losing a
// proof versus telling a lie.
~pub tor write { block: *Block<!raw|!live>, bytes: string }
| ok *Block<live!>
| rejected { block: *Block<raw!>, reason: string }

~proc write|zig {
    if (bytes.len == 0) {
        return .{ .rejected = .{ .block = block, .reason = "write of zero bytes" } };
    }
    if (bytes.len > block.cap) {
        return .{ .rejected = .{ .block = block, .reason = "write is larger than the block" } };
    }
    const region = regionOf(block);
    for (0..bytes.len) |i| region[i] = bytes[i];
    block.used = bytes.len;
    return .{ .ok = block };
}

// Borrow `len` bytes from offset `at`. `bytes` is a BORROW of the block's own
// memory — valid until the next `write`, `resize` or `free`, never freed by the
// caller, never a second obligation. Same discipline as blk's staging buffer and
// gzip's output buffer.
//
// Accepts ONLY <!live>. This is the gate: a block straight out of `take` is
// <raw!> and cannot reach this tor. No C call.
//
// The bound is `used`, not `cap`. Reading past what was written is refused, and
// that refusal is an ADDITION over the C rather than a lifted assertion.
~pub tor read { block: *Block<!live>, at: u64, len: u64 }
| view { block: *Block<live!>, bytes: string }
| rejected { block: *Block<live!>, reason: string }

~proc read|zig {
    if (len == 0) {
        return .{ .rejected = .{ .block = block, .reason = "read of zero bytes" } };
    }
    const from: usize = @intCast(at);
    const count: usize = @intCast(len);
    const end = from + count;
    if (end < from or end > block.used) {
        return .{ .rejected = .{ .block = block, .reason = "read runs past the initialized region of the block" } };
    }
    const region = regionOf(block);
    return .{ .view = .{ .block = block, .bytes = region[from..end] } };
}

// THE NAMED ESCAPE from the asymmetry gate.
//
// `free` accepts only <!live>, and only `write`, `read`, `resize` or
// `take.zeroed` mint it, so memory allocated and never touched cannot be
// released. That is the point — but a program that allocates to prove the
// allocation succeeds (a heap probe, a size test, a benchmark's warm-up, a
// reservation held so a later allocation cannot take the run) is legitimate, and
// this is how it says so out loud.
//
// It runs NO C CALL. That is the honest emission: nothing happened, and the
// object file says nothing happened. What it changes is what the compiler will
// let you write next, and what `grep` can find.
//
//     grep -r "alloc:untouched"
//
// See the header note for why the word is `untouched` and not `unused`.
~pub tor untouched { block: *Block<!raw> } -> *Block<live!>

~proc untouched|zig {
    return block;
}

// ============================================================================
// RESIZE
// ============================================================================

// `uk_realloc`, with the ownership rule the C states in prose and cannot check.
//
// alloc.h:211 — "The value of `ptr` should no longer be used after this call
// completes successfully." Consuming the block makes the pre-resize binding
// dead, so a use-after-realloc does not compile
// (tests/negative_use_after_resize.kz).
//
// The other half is the one C gets wrong far more often: on FAILURE the old
// block is still live and still owed a free, and `p = uk_realloc(a, p, n)`
// throws away the only pointer to it. `| refused` is the only place the original
// comes back, so it cannot be dropped by accident. (See README.md — the
// compiler's DISCHARGE wall does not currently police that arm. The STATE wall
// does, and the arm still cannot be ignored.)
//
// WHY THIS ONE TAKES <!live> AND NOT <!raw|!live>. `uk_realloc`'s contract is
// "resize ... while keeping its contents" (alloc.h:207), and
// `uk_realloc_ifpages` really does copy `min(size, mallocsize)` bytes across
// (alloc.c:225-230). That contract only means something if there are contents.
// Requiring <!live> is what lets BOTH arms mint <live!> truthfully — on success
// the copied prefix is initialized and `used` is clamped to it, on failure the
// original was never touched. A raw block that needs to be bigger wants a bigger
// `take`, which is one call cheaper and copies nothing.
//
// A zero-byte resize is REFUSED before the C is called. `uk_realloc_ifpages`
// treats `!size` as free-and-return-NULL (alloc.c:216-219), which from this side
// is indistinguishable from a failed grow — and taking that arm would hand back
// a pointer the C has already freed. This is the one place in ukalloc where a
// NULL return means two opposite things, and the lift refuses to let it.
//
// AN ALIGNED BLOCK IS GROWN BY ALLOCATE-COPY-FREE, not by realloc: realloc does
// not preserve alignment and POSIX has no `aligned_realloc`. That is the same
// sequence a careful C author writes by hand; it is not something this lift
// added, and the alignment the caller asked for survives the resize.
~pub tor resize { block: *Block<!live>, bytes: u64 }
| ok *Block<live!>
| refused { block: *Block<live!>, bytes: u64, reason: string }

~proc resize|zig {
    if (bytes == 0) {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .block = block, .bytes = bytes, .reason = "resize to zero bytes: uk_realloc would free the block and report it as a failed allocation (alloc.c:216); call free instead" } };
    }
    const want: usize = @intCast(bytes);
    const a = block.heap;
    const keep_used = if (block.used > want) want else block.used;

    if (block.alignment != 0) {
        const al: usize = block.alignment;
        const off = alignUp(HEADER, al);
        const total = off + want;
        if (total < want) {
            return .{ .refused = .{ .block = block, .bytes = bytes, .reason = "allocation size overflows the address space once the aligned block header is added" } };
        }
        var out: ?*anyopaque = null;
        const rc = uk_posix_memalign_ifpages(a, &out, al, total);
        if (rc != 0) {
            return .{ .refused = .{ .block = block, .bytes = bytes, .reason = "uk_posix_memalign_ifpages could not grow the aligned block; the original is untouched and still owed" } };
        }
        const raw = out orelse {
            return .{ .refused = .{ .block = block, .bytes = bytes, .reason = "uk_posix_memalign_ifpages reported success with a NULL pointer; the original is untouched and still owed" } };
        };
        const src = regionOf(block);
        const nb = publish(a, raw, off, want, keep_used, al);
        const dst = regionOf(nb);
        for (0..keep_used) |i| dst[i] = src[i];
        uk_free_ifpages(a, @ptrCast(block));
        return .{ .ok = nb };
    }

    const total = HEADER + want;
    if (total < want) {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .block = block, .bytes = bytes, .reason = "allocation size overflows the address space once the block header is added" } };
    }
    // uk_realloc_ifpages copies min(new, old) bytes from the base, so the header
    // travels with the payload and only the two size fields need rewriting.
    const moved = uk_realloc_ifpages(a, @ptrCast(block), total) orelse {
        return .{ .refused = .{ .block = block, .bytes = bytes, .reason = "uk_realloc_ifpages returned NULL; the original block is untouched and still owed" } };
    };
    const nb: *Block = @ptrCast(@alignCast(moved));
    nb.cap = want;
    nb.used = keep_used;
    return .{ .ok = nb };
}

// ============================================================================
// RELEASE
// ============================================================================

// `uk_free`. Accepts ONLY <!live> — the asymmetry — and consumes it, which makes
// double-free unspellable.
//
// The allocator comes off the block, so there is no argument to get wrong, and
// alloc.h:298's "must have been obtained from ... the same allocator" is
// structural. `uk_free_ifpages` finds its metadata by page-aligning the pointer
// down (alloc.c:99-121), which is why one `free` serves both `take` and
// `take.aligned` even though their bases sit differently inside their pages.
//
// This retires alloc.c:109's `UK_ASSERT(ptr >= __PAGE_SIZE + 32)` outright. That
// assertion is ukalloc's only defence against being handed a pointer it did not
// allocate, it is a plausibility test rather than a proof, and it is
// `do {} while(0)` in every shipped image. Here the only sources of a `*Block`
// are the three `take`s. A stack address, a string literal, an interior pointer
// or an integer cast to a pointer are not programs that exist.
//
// No failure arm: `uk_free_ifpages` returns void and cannot fail. Inventing one
// would be a lie about what the C does.
~pub tor free { block: *Block<!live> }

~proc free|zig {
    uk_free_ifpages(block.heap, @ptrCast(block));
}
